  {slug:"senegambia",name:"Senegambia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/senegambia.jpg",desc:"Senegambia is the region of West Africa between the Senegal and Gambia rivers. It was dominated for a time by the Jolof Empire and was later home to the Kaabu Empire. It became an early zone of Atlantic trade, including the slave trade, with European merchants."},
  {slug:"abbasid-caliphate",name:"Abbasid Caliphate",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate.jpg",desc:"The Abbasid Caliphate was the Islamic caliphate that replaced the Umayyads in 750 and ruled from Baghdad, which it founded as its capital. Its early centuries were a golden age of trade, scholarship and science, but its political authority later fragmented among regional dynasties such as the…"},
  {slug:"herat",name:"Herat",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/herat.jpg",desc:"Herat is a city in western Afghanistan, historically a major centre of Khorasan. Under the Timurid Empire, especially in the fifteenth century, it became a capital and a leading centre of Persian art, literature and scholarship. It was the home of the Herat school of miniature painting."},
  {slug:"hongwu-emperor",name:"Hongwu Emperor",type:"person",dates:"founded dynasty 1368",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor.jpg",desc:"The Hongwu Emperor, born Zhu Yuanzhang, was a peasant-born rebel leader who founded the Ming dynasty in 1368. He drove the Mongol Yuan court out of China, ending the Yuan Dynasty. As emperor he built a highly centralised and autocratic state, laying the foundations of the Ming Empire."},
  {slug:"beijing",name:"Beijing",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/beijing.jpg",desc:"Beijing is a city in northern China that has served as the capital of several imperial dynasties. As Dadu (Khanbaliq) it was the capital of the Yuan Dynasty, and the Ming Empire moved its capital there in the early fifteenth century, building the Forbidden City. Its fall to rebels and then to the…"},
  {slug:"luba-empire",name:"Luba Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/luba-empire.jpg",desc:"The Luba Empire was a Central African state in the Upemba region of the south-eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, flourishing from about the sixteenth to the late nineteenth century. It was built around a sacred kingship and a network of tributary chiefdoms, with institutions such as the…"},
  {slug:"free-imperial-city",name:"Free imperial city",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city.jpg",desc:"A free imperial city was a city in the Holy Roman Empire that was subject only to the emperor rather than a territorial prince. Such cities had self-government and representation in the Imperial Diet. Most lost their status in the Napoleonic reorganisation, though a few survived as city-states…"},
  {slug:"paropamisadae",name:"Paropamisadae",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/paropamisadae.jpg",desc:"Paropamisadae was an ancient province in the Hindu Kush region of present-day Afghanistan, around the area of modern Kabul. It was among the eastern satrapies of Alexander's empire inherited by Seleucus I. Following his conflict with Chandragupta Maurya, Seleucus ceded these eastern territories…"},
  {slug:"johannes-gutenberg",name:"Johannes Gutenberg",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg.jpg",desc:"Johannes Gutenberg was a fifteenth-century German craftsman who developed printing with movable metal type in Europe. His press transformed the spread of knowledge in Europe. In China, Bi Sheng had invented movable type around 1040 CE under the Song Dynasty, about four centuries earlier."},
  {slug:"ethiopian-highlands",name:"Ethiopian highlands",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands.jpg",desc:"The Ethiopian highlands are a large mountainous plateau in the Horn of Africa. Their fertile land and natural defences made them the heartland of the Aksumite Empire and its Christian successors, and their invasion by the Adal Sultanate in the sixteenth century came close to destroying the…"},
  {slug:"bosphorus",name:"Bosphorus",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/bosphorus.jpg",desc:"The Bosphorus is a narrow strait in northwestern Turkey connecting the Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara and separating Europe from Asia. Constantinople stood on its European shore, controlling traffic between the two seas. This strategic position underlay the foundation and long survival of the…"},
  {slug:"keira-dynasty",name:"Keira dynasty",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/keira-dynasty.jpg",desc:"The Keira dynasty was the ruling dynasty of the Darfur Sultanate, founded in the seventeenth century by Sulayman Solong. It ruled until the defeat of Sultan Ibrahim in 1874. It was restored under Ali Dinar after 1898 until Darfur's incorporation into Anglo-Egyptian Sudan in 1916."},
  {slug:"mississippi-river",name:"Mississippi River",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/mississippi-river.jpg",desc:"The Mississippi is the principal river of North America, draining a vast basin into the Gulf of Mexico. French explorers travelled it in the seventeenth century and claimed its basin as Louisiana for the French Empire. New Orleans, founded at its mouth, became the colony's capital."},
  {slug:"suppiluliuma-i",name:"Suppiluliuma I",type:"person",dates:"c. 1344–1322 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i.jpg",desc:"Suppiluliuma I was a king of the Hittite Empire who reigned c. 1344–1322 BCE. He expanded Hittite power into Syria and destroyed the Mitanni Empire. The Mitanni Empire profile notes that he sacked Washukanni and installed Shattiwaza as a vassal king."},
  {slug:"yuan-dynasty",name:"Yuan Dynasty",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty.jpg",desc:"The Yuan Dynasty was the Mongol-ruled dynasty of China, proclaimed by Kublai Khan in 1271, which completed the conquest of the Southern Song in 1279. It made Beijing, then Dadu, its capital, promoted trade across Eurasia and patronised Tibetan Buddhism. Weakened by factional conflict, floods,…"},
  {slug:"persia",name:"Persia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/persia.jpg",desc:"Persia is the historical name for Iran, derived from the region of Pars in the south-west. It was the heartland of the Achaemenid, Parthian and Sasanian empires and later of the Safavid, Afsharid and Zand dynasties. Conquered by the Macedonian Empire, the Umayyad Caliphate and the Mongols, it…"},
  {slug:"mashhad",name:"Mashhad",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/mashhad.jpg",desc:"Mashhad is a city in north-eastern Iran, in Khorasan, and an important Shia pilgrimage centre around the shrine of Imam Reza. Nader Shah made it a principal seat of the Afsharid Empire. After Nader's assassination in 1747, his grandson Shahrukh held a reduced Afsharid realm centred on Mashhad."},
  {slug:"sargon-ii",name:"Sargon II",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sargon-ii.jpg",desc:"Sargon II was a king of the Neo-Assyrian Empire in the late eighth century BCE, who founded a new capital at Dur-Sharrukin. His reign saw extensive campaigns across the Near East. The Elamite Confederation profile notes that the Elamite king Humban-nikash I defeated him at the Battle of Der in…"},
  {slug:"pacific-ocean",name:"Pacific Ocean",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean.jpg",desc:"The Pacific Ocean is the largest ocean on Earth, between Asia and Australia to the west and the Americas to the east. Spain crossed it to establish the Manila galleon trade, and the Russian Empire expanded to its northern shores. The German Empire held Pacific island colonies, and the Empire of…"},
  {slug:"dutch-east-india-company",name:"Dutch East India Company",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company.jpg",desc:"The Dutch East India Company (VOC) was a chartered trading company founded in 1602, with powers to wage war, make treaties and govern territory in Asia. It was the main instrument of the Dutch Empire in the East Indies, displaced the Portuguese Empire from much of the spice trade and served as a…"},
  {slug:"babylonia",name:"Babylonia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/babylonia.jpg",desc:"Babylonia was the region of southern and central Mesopotamia centred on the city of Babylon. Its fertile irrigated land made it one of the wealthiest regions of the ancient Near East. It was ruled successively by the Babylonian kings, the Assyrians, the Chaldeans, the Achaemenid Empire and the…"},
  {slug:"haile-selassie",name:"Haile Selassie",type:"person",dates:"d. 1975",img:"/images/encyclopedia/haile-selassie.jpg",desc:"Haile Selassie was the last Emperor of Ethiopia, who died in 1975. He went into exile after the Italian invasion of 1935 and was restored in 1941 when the Italian Empire's East African holdings collapsed. He pursued modernising reforms and became a prominent international figure, but was deposed…"},
  {slug:"galilee",name:"Galilee",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/galilee.jpg",desc:"Galilee is a region in the north of present-day Israel. Saladin's victory over the Crusaders at Hattin in 1187, near the Sea of Galilee, was the foundation of the Ayyubid Empire's reconquest, and the Mamluk victory over the Mongols at Ain Jalut in 1260 in Galilee set a lasting western frontier…"},
  {slug:"indian-rebellion-of-1857",name:"Indian Rebellion of 1857",type:"event",dates:"1857",img:"/images/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857.jpg",desc:"The Indian Rebellion of 1857 was a major uprising against the rule of the British East India Company, beginning with a mutiny of Indian soldiers. The rebels proclaimed the elderly Mughal emperor Bahadur Shah II as their leader. After its suppression the British exiled him, formally ending the…"},
  {slug:"nabonidus",name:"Nabonidus",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus.jpg",desc:"Nabonidus was the last king of the Chaldean or Neo-Babylonian Empire. He alienated Babylon's religious establishment by neglecting the cult of Marduk and spent about a decade at the Arabian oasis of Tayma, during which the New Year festival was not held. His unpopularity contributed to the…"},
  {slug:"north-sea",name:"North Sea",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/north-sea.jpg",desc:"The North Sea is a marginal sea of the Atlantic Ocean between Britain, Scandinavia, Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium and France. Its storms are notoriously unpredictable. After the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588, North Sea storms wrecked many of its ships, demonstrating the limits of the…"},
  {slug:"second-world-war",name:"Second World War",type:"event",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg",desc:"The Second World War was the global conflict of 1939 to 1945 between the Allies and the Axis powers, the deadliest war in history. It destroyed the Italian Empire, whose African colonies were lost to Allied forces, and fatally weakened the British Empire, accelerating decolonisation. The…"},
  {slug:"fall-of-nineveh",name:"Fall of Nineveh",type:"event",dates:"612 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-nineveh.jpg",desc:"The fall of Nineveh was the destruction of the Assyrian capital in 612 BCE by a coalition of Medes and Babylonians. It was the decisive blow to the Assyrian Empire and a founding moment of Median power. The event also appears in the Sasanian Empire profile as part of the long history of the region."},
  {slug:"nineveh",name:"Nineveh",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/nineveh.jpg",desc:"Nineveh was an ancient city on the Tigris opposite modern Mosul, and the last great capital of the Assyrian Empire. Ashurbanipal assembled his famous library of cuneiform tablets there. Its capture and destruction in 612 BCE by a coalition of the Median Empire and Babylonians led by Nabopolassar…"},
  {slug:"ghana",name:"Ghana",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ghana.jpg",desc:"Ghana is a country in West Africa, independent from Britain in 1957 and the first sub-Saharan colony to gain independence. It was the home of the Ashanti Empire and took its name from the medieval Ghana Empire, which lay far to the north-west in present-day Mauritania and Mali."},
  {slug:"tigris",name:"Tigris",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/tigris.jpg",desc:"The Tigris is one of the two great rivers of Mesopotamia, flowing from eastern Anatolia south-east through Iraq to join the Euphrates near the Persian Gulf. Its waters supported irrigation agriculture and the cities of Assur, Nineveh, Ctesiphon, Seleucia and Baghdad. It runs through the history…"},
  {slug:"songhai-empire",name:"Songhai Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/songhai-empire.jpg",desc:"The Songhai Empire was a West African state centred on Gao on the middle Niger, which expanded in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under Sunni Ali and Askia Muhammad. It controlled Timbuktu, Djenné and the trans-Saharan trade and became one of the largest states in African history. It was…"},
  {slug:"qadi",name:"Qadi",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/qadi.jpg",desc:"A qadi is a judge in Islamic law, responsible for applying sharia in civil, family and criminal cases. Qadis were appointed by Muslim rulers from the Rashidun and Umayyad caliphates onwards. They served in the judicial systems of states such as the Mamluk Sultanate, the Safavid Empire, the…"},
  {slug:"kingdom-of-castile",name:"Kingdom of Castile",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile.jpg",desc:"The Kingdom of Castile was a Christian kingdom of central Iberia and the leading power of the Reconquista. It fought both the Almoravid Empire and the Almohad Caliphate, notably at Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212. United dynastically with Aragon through Isabella and Ferdinand, Castile sponsored the…"},
  {slug:"guinea-bissau",name:"Guinea-Bissau",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau.jpg",desc:"Guinea-Bissau is a country on the West African coast. Part of its territory corresponds to the heartland of the Kaabu Empire, which emerged as an outlying province of the Mali Empire. It later became a Portuguese colony, and Portuguese remains its official language, part of the linguistic legacy…"},
  {slug:"nile",name:"Nile",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/nile.jpg",desc:"The Nile is the longest river in Africa, flowing north through Sudan and Egypt to the Mediterranean. Its annual flood sustained the agricultural economy of Egypt and Nubia and made the valley one of the richest regions of the ancient and medieval world. It was the foundation of the Egyptian New…"},
  {slug:"india",name:"India",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/india.jpg",desc:"India is a region of South Asia, home to some of the world's oldest civilisations and a long series of empires, including the Maurya, Gupta, Delhi Sultanate, Mughal, Maratha and Sikh states. Its wealth and trade drew the attention of many outside powers. It was invaded by the Macedonian and…"},
  {slug:"rabih-az-zubayr",name:"Rabih az-Zubayr",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr.jpg",desc:"Rabih az-Zubayr was a Sudanese warlord and slave trader who built a powerful state in the Lake Chad region in the late nineteenth century. He conquered Bornu in 1893 and threatened Wadai. He was killed by French forces in 1900, but his advance was the first major external threat to reach the…"},
  {slug:"battle-of-gaugamela",name:"Battle of Gaugamela",type:"event",dates:"331 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela.jpg",desc:"The Battle of Gaugamela was fought in 331 BCE in northern Mesopotamia between Alexander the Great and the Achaemenid king Darius III. Despite being outnumbered, Alexander won a decisive victory and Darius fled. The battle led to the fall of the Achaemenid Empire and its conquest by the Macedonian…"},
  {slug:"libya",name:"Libya",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/libya.jpg",desc:"Libya is a country in North Africa on the Mediterranean coast, comprising the historical regions of Tripolitania, Cyrenaica and the Fezzan. The Fezzan was the heartland of the Garamantian Empire, and the coast was conquered by Arab armies under the Rashidun Caliphate. Libya was a colony of the…"},
  {slug:"herodotus",name:"Herodotus",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg",desc:"Herodotus was a Greek historian of the fifth century BCE, often called the father of history, whose Histories describe the peoples of the Persian world and the Greco-Persian Wars. He is a key source for the Median Empire, crediting Deioces with its unification. He also described the Garamantes of…"},
  {slug:"sasanian-empire",name:"Sasanian Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire.jpg",desc:"The Sasanian Empire was the last pre-Islamic Persian empire, founded by Ardashir I in the third century CE after overthrowing the Parthian Empire. Centred on Ctesiphon, it was a great rival of Rome and Byzantium, with Zoroastrianism as its state religion. It fell to the Arab conquests of the…"},
  {slug:"darius-i",name:"Darius I",type:"person",dates:"r. 522–486 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg",desc:"Darius I, also known as Darius the Great, was king of the Achaemenid Empire (r. 522–486 BCE). He reorganised the empire into satrapies with regular tribute, introduced the daric coinage, developed the Royal Road and built at Persepolis and Susa. His reign marked the administrative consolidation…"},
  {slug:"damascus",name:"Damascus",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/damascus.jpg",desc:"Damascus is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, now the capital of Syria. It was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate, whose Great Mosque still stands there. It was later contested by the Fatimid Caliphate and served as a major centre of the Ayyubid Empire, where Saladin…"},
  {slug:"durrani-empire",name:"Durrani Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/durrani-empire.jpg",desc:"The Durrani Empire was an Afghan state founded by Ahmad Shah Durrani in 1747 after the death of Nader Shah of the Afsharid Empire. It raided northern India repeatedly, defeated the Maratha Empire at the Third Battle of Panipat in 1761 and further weakened the Mughal Empire. Its loss of the Punjab…"},
  {slug:"walata",name:"Walata",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/walata.jpg",desc:"Walata, or Oualata, is an oasis town in south-eastern Mauritania that was an important terminus of trans-Saharan caravan routes. The nearby escarpment of Dhar Walata is associated with the Tichitt culture from which the Ghana Empire emerged. The Mali Empire profile notes that Tuareg forces seized…"},
  {slug:"charles-v-holy-roman-emperor",name:"Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg",desc:"Charles V was a Habsburg ruler who became king of Spain as Charles I in 1516 and Holy Roman Emperor in 1519. He inherited the Spanish, Burgundian and Habsburg lands, presiding over the Spanish conquest of the Aztec and Inca empires. His reign marked the height of the Spanish Empire's European and…"},
  {slug:"golden-horde",name:"Golden Horde",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/golden-horde.jpg",desc:"The Golden Horde was the Mongol khanate in the western steppe, founded by descendants of Genghis Khan's son Jochi. It ruled the Pontic–Caspian steppe and held the Rus' principalities in tributary subjection from the thirteenth century, forming one of the successor states of the Mongol Empire."},
  {slug:"seville",name:"Seville",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/seville.jpg",desc:"Seville is a city in Andalusia in southern Spain, on the Guadalquivir River. It served as a capital of the Almohad Caliphate in al-Andalus, where the Almohads built the great mosque whose minaret survives as the Giralda. It later became the monopoly port for Spanish trade with the Americas, as…"},
  {slug:"crimea",name:"Crimea",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/crimea.jpg",desc:"Crimea is a peninsula on the northern coast of the Black Sea. It was ruled by a succession of steppe powers and later by the Crimean Khanate, a vassal of the Ottoman Empire from the fifteenth century. The Russian Empire annexed Crimea in 1783 and fought the Crimean War there in the 1850s."},
  {slug:"gentry",name:"Gentry",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/gentry.jpg",desc:"The gentry in Ming China were the local elite of landowners and degree holders who had passed the civil service examinations. They dominated local society, mediated between the state and villages, and were central to the social order of the Ming Empire."},
  {slug:"qing-empire",name:"Qing Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/qing-empire.jpg",desc:"The Qing Empire was the last imperial dynasty of China, founded by the Manchus, who conquered Ming China from 1644 and ruled until 1912. It expanded China's territory to include Mongolia, Xinjiang and Tibet and presided over great population and commercial growth. Weakened in the nineteenth…"},
  {slug:"mehmed-ii",name:"Mehmed II",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii.jpg",desc:"Mehmed II was an Ottoman sultan who conquered Constantinople in 1453, ending the Byzantine Empire. Known as the Conqueror, he made the city the Ottoman capital and expanded the empire in the Balkans and Anatolia. His reign established the Ottoman Empire as a major world power."},
  {slug:"spain",name:"Spain",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/spain.jpg",desc:"Spain is a country on the Iberian Peninsula. Parts of it were ruled by Carthage, Rome and Islamic states such as the Umayyads and Almoravids before the Christian kingdoms completed the Reconquista. United Spain built the Spanish Empire, conquering the Aztec Empire, and competed with Portugal,…"},
  {slug:"kebra-nagast",name:"Kebra Nagast",type:"work",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast.jpg",desc:"The Kebra Nagast, or Glory of the Kings, is an Ethiopian text compiled in Ge'ez in the medieval period. It tells of the Queen of Sheba, King Solomon and their son Menelik I, and the bringing of the Ark of the Covenant to Ethiopia. It provided the Solomonic legitimacy of the Ethiopian Empire's…"},
  {slug:"ibn-battuta",name:"Ibn Battuta",type:"person",dates:"1334–1341 at Delhi",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta.jpg",desc:"Ibn Battuta was a fourteenth-century Moroccan traveller whose account of his journeys across Africa and Asia is a major historical source. He served at the court of Muhammad bin Tughluq in Delhi between 1334 and 1341, including as a judge. His observations are a valuable record of the Delhi…"},
  {slug:"pope-leo-iii",name:"Pope Leo III",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii.jpg",desc:"Pope Leo III was bishop of Rome from 795 to 816. On Christmas Day 800 CE he crowned Charlemagne Emperor of the Romans in St Peter's Basilica. The coronation established the Carolingian Empire's imperial title and was later regarded as the origin of the Holy Roman Empire."},
  {slug:"tercio",name:"Tercio",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/tercio.jpg",desc:"The tercio was a Spanish infantry formation of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries that combined pikemen, swordsmen and arquebusiers in large, disciplined squares. It made the armies of the Spanish Empire among the most formidable in Europe. The Swedish Empire profile notes that Gustavus…"},
  {slug:"euclid",name:"Euclid",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/euclid.jpg",desc:"Euclid was a Greek mathematician of Alexandria, active around 300 BCE, whose Elements became the foundational textbook of geometry. His work was translated into Arabic during the translation movement of the Abbasid Caliphate, preserving and transmitting it to later Islamic and European scholars."},
  {slug:"kushite-empire",name:"Kushite Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kushite-empire.jpg",desc:"The Kushite Empire, or Kingdom of Kush, was a Nubian state on the middle Nile in present-day Sudan, centred successively on Kerma, Napata and Meroe. Long under Egyptian New Kingdom rule, Kush later conquered Egypt and ruled it as the Twenty-Fifth Dynasty in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE.…"},
  {slug:"transoxiana",name:"Transoxiana",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/transoxiana.jpg",desc:"Transoxiana is the historical name for the region of Central Asia between the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers, roughly corresponding to modern Uzbekistan and neighbouring areas. Its oasis cities, including Samarkand and Bukhara, were centres of trade and learning on the Silk Road. It was the…"},
  {slug:"samanid-empire",name:"Samanid Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/samanid-empire.jpg",desc:"The Samanid Empire was a Persian dynasty that ruled Khurasan and Transoxiana in the ninth and tenth centuries, initially as governors under the Abbasid Caliphate. Its capital Bukhara became a centre of the revival of Persian language and literature. The Abbasid Caliphate profile lists the…"},
  {slug:"manila",name:"Manila",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/manila.jpg",desc:"Manila is the capital of the Philippines, on Manila Bay in Luzon. The Spanish established it as the colonial capital of the Philippines in 1571. It became the Asian terminus of the Manila galleon trade, linking Spanish America to China and making Manila a centre of the Spanish Empire's Pacific…"},
  {slug:"baghdad",name:"Baghdad",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/baghdad.jpg",desc:"Baghdad is a city on the Tigris in Iraq, founded in 762 by the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur as the capital of the Abbasid Caliphate. It became one of the largest cities in the world and a great centre of trade and learning. It came under Seljuk control in 1055 and was sacked by the Mongols in 1258,…"},
  {slug:"moravia",name:"Moravia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/moravia.jpg",desc:"Moravia is a historical region in the eastern part of the present-day Czech Republic. It was a crown land of the Habsburg monarchy and part of the Austrian half of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. Together with Bohemia it formed one of the empire's most industrialised areas, with textile, coal and…"},
  {slug:"kertanegara",name:"Kertanegara",type:"person",dates:"c. 1275",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kertanegara.jpg",desc:"Kertanegara was the last king of Singhasari in eastern Java. Around c. 1275 he launched a major military expedition against Sumatra, further weakening the Srivijaya Empire. His defiance of Kublai Khan led to a Mongol expedition to Java, during which Majapahit emerged."},
  {slug:"hong-kong",name:"Hong Kong",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hong-kong.jpg",desc:"Hong Kong is a territory on the southern coast of China. It was ceded to Britain after the First Opium War, a symbol of the Qing Empire's humiliation by Western powers. It developed into a major British colonial port, and its handover to China in 1997 is often taken as the end of the British Empire."},
  {slug:"strait-of-malacca",name:"Strait of Malacca",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca.jpg",desc:"The Strait of Malacca is the channel between the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra, one of the busiest sea routes in the world. Srivijaya controlled it, taxing trade between the Indian Ocean and China. The Majapahit Empire profile notes the later rise of Malacca."},
  {slug:"central-asia",name:"Central Asia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/central-asia.jpg",desc:"Central Asia is the vast inland region between the Caspian Sea and western China, including the steppes and the oasis cities of Transoxiana such as Samarkand and Bukhara. It was a crossroads of the Silk Road and the homeland or battleground of many empires, from the Achaemenid and Seleucid…"},
  {slug:"russia",name:"Russia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/russia.jpg",desc:"Russia is a vast country spanning eastern Europe and northern Asia, which grew from the principality of Moscow into the Russian Empire and later the Soviet Union. As a major power it fought repeatedly against the Swedish Empire, the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the Ottoman Empire, and later…"},
  {slug:"akitu",name:"Akitu",type:"event",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/akitu.jpg",desc:"Akitu was the Mesopotamian New Year festival, celebrated most famously at Babylon in honour of the god Marduk. It included processions, religious rites and a ritual renewal of the king's authority. It was an important part of Babylonian royal and religious ideology."},
  {slug:"maurya-empire",name:"Maurya Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/maurya-empire.jpg",desc:"The Maurya Empire was the first state to unite most of the Indian subcontinent, founded by Chandragupta Maurya around 321 BCE. Under Ashoka it reached its greatest extent, and Ashoka's edicts promoted dhamma and Buddhist ethics. It had a centralised administration described in the Arthashastra…"},
  {slug:"catherine-the-great",name:"Catherine the Great",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great.jpg",desc:"Catherine the Great, or Catherine II, was empress of Russia from 1762 to 1796. Her reign saw major territorial expansion, including gains on the Black Sea coast and Crimea from the Ottomans. She took part in the three partitions of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, which erased it from the map,…"},
  {slug:"departement",name:"Département",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/departement.jpg",desc:"A département is an administrative division of France created in 1790 to replace the old provinces with uniform units. The French Empire extended the system to conquered lands and to Algeria after 1848. The term 'department' also describes specialised administrative bodies in other states, such…"},
  {slug:"rozvi-empire",name:"Rozvi Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire.jpg",desc:"The Rozvi Empire was a state on the Zimbabwean plateau that emerged in the late seventeenth century under the Changamire dynasty, after breaking away from and overshadowing the Mutapa state. Its economy rested on cattle-based patronage, the gold and ivory trade and tribute from a predominantly…"},
  {slug:"aleppo",name:"Aleppo",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/aleppo.jpg",desc:"Aleppo is a major city in northern Syria with a long history as a trading and political centre. It was a key prize in the twelfth-century struggles of Nur al-Din and Saladin, whose control of it was central to the foundation of the Ayyubid Empire. Ayyubid princes ruled it until the Mongol sack of…"},
  {slug:"great-northern-war",name:"Great Northern War",type:"event",dates:"1700–1721",img:"/images/encyclopedia/great-northern-war.jpg",desc:"The Great Northern War (1700–1721) was fought between Sweden and a coalition led by Russia. After early Swedish successes, Charles XII's defeat at Poltava in 1709 broke Swedish power, and the Treaty of Nystad in 1721 transferred Baltic provinces to Russia, ending the Swedish Empire and…"},
  {slug:"pyrenees",name:"Pyrenees",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/pyrenees.jpg",desc:"The Pyrenees are a mountain range separating the Iberian Peninsula from France. After conquering Spain from 711 CE, Umayyad forces crossed the Pyrenees into Francia. Their advance was halted at the Battle of Tours or Poitiers in 732 CE, marking the limit of the Umayyad Caliphate's expansion in…"},
  {slug:"arabia",name:"Arabia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/arabia.jpg",desc:"Arabia is the region of the Arabian Peninsula in south-west Asia, bounded by the Red Sea, the Arabian Sea and the Persian Gulf. It was the birthplace of Islam and the starting point of the Rashidun Caliphate's conquests. Its ports and incense routes linked it to the Aksumite, Babylonian and…"},
  {slug:"arsacid-dynasty",name:"Arsacid dynasty",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty.jpg",desc:"The Arsacid dynasty was the ruling family of the Parthian Empire, founded by Arsaces I in the third century BCE. It ruled Iran and Mesopotamia through a relatively decentralised system relying on powerful noble families and vassal kings. It was overthrown by Ardashir I in 224 CE, giving way to…"},
  {slug:"senegal-river",name:"Senegal River",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/senegal-river.jpg",desc:"The Senegal River flows from the Guinea highlands to the Atlantic, forming much of the border between modern Senegal and Mauritania. Its valley was an important agricultural and trading zone at the southern edge of the Sahara. The Almoravid Empire and Mali Empire profiles refer to it in…"},
  {slug:"emperor-taizong-of-tang",name:"Emperor Taizong of Tang",type:"person",dates:"r. 626–649",img:"/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang.jpg",desc:"Emperor Taizong, born Li Shimin, was the second emperor of the Tang Dynasty (r. 626–649). He defeated the Eastern Turkic Khaganate, expanded Tang power into Central Asia and consolidated the institutions of government. He is remembered as one of China's greatest rulers."},
  {slug:"persepolis",name:"Persepolis",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg",desc:"Persepolis was the ceremonial capital of the Achaemenid Empire, located in Fars in south-western Iran and begun under Darius I. Subject nations presented tribute there, as depicted in the reliefs of its palace complex. Alexander the Great occupied it and burned the palace in 330 BCE, and its…"},
  {slug:"morocco",name:"Morocco",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/morocco.jpg",desc:"Morocco is a country in north-west Africa with Atlantic and Mediterranean coasts. It was the heartland of the Almoravid Empire and the Almohad Caliphate, whose capital was Marrakesh. In 1591 a Moroccan army destroyed the Songhai Empire, and Morocco's trade routes linked it to the Ghana and Darfur…"},
  {slug:"kaidu",name:"Kaidu",type:"person",dates:"1272–1301",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kaidu.jpg",desc:"Kaidu was a Mongol prince, a grandson of Ögedei, who dominated Central Asia from 1272 to 1301. Allied with Duwa of the Chagatai Khanate, he waged long wars against Kublai Khan and the Yuan Dynasty. His opposition helped divide the Mongol world into competing khanates."},
  {slug:"papacy",name:"Papacy",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/papacy.jpg",desc:"The papacy is the office of the Pope, the bishop of Rome and head of the Catholic Church. In the eighth and ninth centuries it allied with the Carolingians, culminating in Charlemagne's coronation in 800, and later crowned the Holy Roman emperors. Popes also proclaimed crusades against Muslim…"},
  {slug:"nubia",name:"Nubia",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/nubia.jpg",desc:"Nubia is the region along the Nile south of Egypt, covering southern Egypt and northern Sudan. It was rich in gold and controlled trade routes to tropical Africa. The Egyptian New Kingdom conquered it and governed it through the Viceroy of Kush, while Nubia was also the homeland of the Kushite…"},
  {slug:"pedro-i-of-brazil",name:"Pedro I of Brazil",type:"person",dates:"abdicated 1831",img:"/images/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil.jpg",desc:"Pedro I was the founder and first emperor of Brazil, a prince of the Portuguese royal house who proclaimed Brazilian independence on 7 September 1822. His reign was turbulent, marked by regional revolts and tension with the Brazilian-born elite. He abdicated in 1831 in favour of his young son…"},
  {slug:"battle-of-the-granicus",name:"Battle of the Granicus",type:"event",dates:"334 BCE",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus.jpg",desc:"The Battle of the Granicus was fought in 334 BCE at the Granicus River in northwestern Anatolia, the first major victory of Alexander the Great over the Persian satraps. It opened Asia Minor to the Macedonian Empire. For the Achaemenid Empire it was the first of the defeats that led to its fall."},
  {slug:"hernan-cortes",name:"Hernán Cortés",type:"person",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg",desc:"Hernán Cortés was a Spanish conquistador who led the expedition that brought down the Aztec Empire between 1519 and 1521. Allied with indigenous peoples such as the Tlaxcalans, he captured the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan. His conquest was a founding event of the Spanish Empire in the Americas."},
  {slug:"karakorum",name:"Karakorum",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/karakorum.jpg",desc:"Karakorum was the capital of the Mongol Empire in central Mongolia, established under Ögedei. It was the seat of the Great Khan. The Chagatai Khanate was subordinate to the Great Khan in Karakorum during Chagatai's lifetime."},
  {slug:"battle-of-shimbra-kure",name:"Battle of Shimbra Kure",type:"event",dates:"1529",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-shimbra-kure.jpg",desc:"The Battle of Shimbra Kure was fought in March 1529 in the Ethiopian highlands, where the forces of the Adal Sultanate under Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi routed a much larger army of the Ethiopian Empire. Ahmad's victory rested on Ottoman-supplied firearms and disciplined use of them. It shattered…"},
  {slug:"twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt",name:"Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg",desc:"The Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt was a line of Kushite kings from Nubia who ruled Egypt in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE. Under rulers such as Piye and Taharqa they united the Nile Valley from Napata to the Delta and revived traditional Egyptian religion and art. They were driven out of…"},
  {slug:"hetman",name:"Hetman",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/hetman.jpg",desc:"Hetman was the title of the highest military commander in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, with separate grand and field hetmans for the Crown and Lithuania. Hetmans held their posts for life and enjoyed considerable independence from the king. The office was a central feature of the…"},
  {slug:"kievan-rus",name:"Kievan Rus'",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kievan-rus.jpg",desc:"Kievan Rus' was a medieval East Slavic state centred on Kyiv, flourishing from the late ninth to the thirteenth century. Its rulers attacked and helped destroy the Khazar Khaganate in the tenth century, removing a major power on the steppe. Fragmented into rival principalities, it was overrun by…"},
  {slug:"marco-polo",name:"Marco Polo",type:"person",dates:"1275–1292 in China",img:"/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo.jpg",desc:"Marco Polo was a Venetian merchant and traveller who lived in China between 1275 and 1292 during the Yuan Dynasty. He served at the court of Kublai Khan and travelled widely in his realm. His account of his travels gave Europeans a detailed picture of Yuan China's wealth and cities."},
  {slug:"isfahan",name:"Isfahan",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/isfahan.jpg",desc:"Isfahan is a city in central Iran with a long history as a political and cultural centre. It was a capital under the Seljuk Empire and was rebuilt as the splendid capital of the Safavid Empire under Shah Abbas I. Its capture by Afghan forces in 1722 ended effective Safavid rule, setting the stage…"},
  {slug:"sosso-empire",name:"Sosso Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sosso-empire.jpg",desc:"The Sosso Empire was a West African state, also known as the kingdom of Kaniaga, that rose as Ghana declined. Under Sumanguru Kanté it dominated the region until its defeat by Sundiata Keita at the Battle of Kirina, which led to the rise of the Mali Empire. The Ghana Empire profile notes it…"},
  {slug:"germany",name:"Germany",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/germany.jpg",desc:"Germany is a country in central Europe. Its lands formed the core of the Holy Roman Empire and were unified in 1871 as the German Empire. Germany was the ally of Austria-Hungary and later of Italy and Japan in the world wars, and it figures in the histories of the Carolingian, Spanish and Swedish…"},
  {slug:"auxilia",name:"Auxilia",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/auxilia.jpg",desc:"The auxilia were the non-citizen troops of the Roman imperial army, recruited from provincial peoples. They provided infantry, cavalry and specialist units such as archers, serving alongside the citizen legions. After long service they received Roman citizenship, which helped integrate…"},
  {slug:"argentina",name:"Argentina",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/argentina.jpg",desc:"Argentina is a country in southern South America that emerged from the Spanish viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata. In the nineteenth century it was a rival of the Brazilian Empire for influence in the Río de la Plata region, notably in the Cisplatine War, and later an ally in the Paraguayan War.…"},
  {slug:"battle-of-yamen",name:"Battle of Yamen",type:"event",dates:"1279",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen.jpg",desc:"The Battle of Yamen was a naval battle fought in March 1279 off the southern coast of China, in which the Yuan fleet destroyed the last loyalist fleet of the Song Dynasty. The young last Song emperor was drowned by his minister to prevent his capture. The battle ended the Song Dynasty and…"},
  {slug:"cuba",name:"Cuba",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/cuba.jpg",desc:"Cuba is the largest island of the Caribbean, colonised by Spain from the early sixteenth century. It remained one of the last colonies of the Spanish Empire until 1898, with a plantation economy built on enslaved African labour. Many enslaved Yoruba from the Oyo region were taken to Cuba, where…"},
  {slug:"kingdom-of-the-lombards",name:"Kingdom of the Lombards",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards.jpg",desc:"The Kingdom of the Lombards was a Germanic kingdom established in Italy after the Lombard invasion of 568. Its pressure on the papacy led popes to seek Frankish protection, and Charlemagne conquered the kingdom in 774. Its absorption was a key step in the formation of the Carolingian Empire, and…"},
  {slug:"al-mamun",name:"al-Ma'mun",type:"person",dates:"r. 813–833",img:"/images/encyclopedia/al-mamun.jpg",desc:"Al-Mamun was an Abbasid caliph who reigned from 813 to 833. He came to power after a civil war against his brother al-Amin. He is famous for his patronage of science and learning, including the translation of Greek works into Arabic, which made Baghdad a leading intellectual centre of the Abbasid…"},
  {slug:"bukhara",name:"Bukhara",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/bukhara.jpg",desc:"Bukhara is a city in present-day Uzbekistan, one of the great oasis centres of the Silk Road and of Islamic learning in Central Asia. It was sacked by the Mongols during the destruction of the Khwarazmian Empire and later fell under the Chagatai and Timurid empires. Nader Shah of the Afsharid…"},
  {slug:"dome-of-the-rock",name:"Dome of the Rock",type:"work",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock.jpg",desc:"The Dome of the Rock is an Islamic shrine on the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, built under the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik in the late seventh century. After Saladin recaptured Jerusalem from the Crusaders in 1187, the Ayyubid Empire restored it as a Muslim holy site, and it remains one of the most…"},
  {slug:"viceroyalty-of-new-spain",name:"Viceroyalty of New Spain",type:"institution",dates:"1535",img:"/images/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain.jpg",desc:"The Viceroyalty of New Spain was the administrative unit of the Spanish Empire covering Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean, parts of what is now the United States and later the Philippines. It was established in 1535 with its capital at Mexico City. The Spanish Empire profile describes it as…"},
  {slug:"sierra-leone",name:"Sierra Leone",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/sierra-leone.jpg",desc:"Sierra Leone is a country on the coast of West Africa. The Punic Empire profile notes that the Carthaginian explorer Hanno the Navigator's voyage around 500 BCE may have reached as far as modern Sierra Leone or Cameroon. The interpretation of his surviving account is debated by scholars."},
  {slug:"meiji-restoration",name:"Meiji Restoration",type:"event",dates:"1868",img:"/images/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration.jpg",desc:"The Meiji Restoration was the political revolution of 1868 that ended the Tokugawa Shogunate and restored direct imperial rule under Emperor Meiji. It launched a rapid programme of modernisation in government, industry and the military. The restoration marked the foundation of the modern Empire…"},
  {slug:"new-kingdom-of-egypt",name:"New Kingdom of Egypt",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt.jpg",desc:"The New Kingdom of Egypt was the period of Egyptian history from about 1550 BCE to about 1070 BCE, covering the Eighteenth to Twentieth Dynasties. After Ahmose I expelled the Hyksos, Egypt became an imperial power, extending its rule into Nubia and the Levant and contending with the Hittite…"},
  {slug:"vizier",name:"Vizier",type:"institution",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/vizier.jpg",desc:"A vizier is a high minister or chief administrator serving a ruler, a title used in many Islamic states and applied by historians to the chief officials of ancient Egypt. In the Abbasid Caliphate the vizier headed the bureaucracy, and the Seljuk vizier Nizam al-Mulk became the model of the…"},
  {slug:"vienna",name:"Vienna",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg",desc:"Vienna is the capital of Austria, on the Danube, and was for centuries the seat of the Habsburg monarchy. It withstood Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683, the second of which was broken with the help of a Polish-Lithuanian relief army under Jan III Sobieski. As capital of the Austro-Hungarian Empire…"},
  {slug:"holy-roman-empire",name:"Holy Roman Empire",type:"polity",dates:"962–1806",img:"/images/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire.jpg",desc:"The Holy Roman Empire was a complex political union of territories in central Europe that existed from 962 to 1806. It claimed succession to the Roman and Carolingian imperial traditions, with an elected emperor presiding over many semi-independent princes and cities. It has its own profile on…"},
  {slug:"the-great-game",name:"The Great Game",type:"event",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/the-great-game.jpg",desc:"The Great Game was the nineteenth-century rivalry between the British and Russian Empires for influence in Central Asia, Afghanistan and Persia. Russian expansion towards Afghanistan brought the two empires close, and the Sikh Empire served as a buffer between British India and the Afghan…"},
  {slug:"battle-of-poltava",name:"Battle of Poltava",type:"event",dates:"1709",img:"/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava.jpg",desc:"The Battle of Poltava was fought in 1709 in present-day Ukraine during the Great Northern War, where the army of Peter I of Russia decisively defeated Charles XII of Sweden. The Swedish army was destroyed and its remnants capitulated soon afterwards. The battle marked the end of the Swedish…"},
  {slug:"atlas-mountains",name:"Atlas Mountains",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains.jpg",desc:"The Atlas Mountains are a mountain range in north-western Africa, running through Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia. The Almohad movement began among the Masmuda Berbers of the High Atlas, at Tinmel, from where it challenged the Almoravid Empire. The mountains also bordered the territories of the…"},
  {slug:"champa",name:"Champa",type:"place",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/champa.jpg",desc:"Champa was a group of kingdoms of the Cham people on the central and southern coast of present-day Vietnam, from the early centuries CE until absorbed by Vietnam. It was a maritime trading power linked to China and India. Fast-ripening rice varieties from Champa transformed agriculture under the…"},
  {slug:"ashanti-empire",name:"Ashanti Empire",type:"polity",dates:"",img:"/images/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire.jpg",desc:"The Ashanti Empire was an Akan state in present-day Ghana, founded by Osei Tutu around the end of the seventeenth century with its capital at Kumasi. Its power rested on gold, trade, including the Atlantic slave trade, and a strong army, and the Golden Stool symbolised its unity. After a series…"}
